/// \defgroup grp_w1_debug_lib EA debug library
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief EA's debug library: the `Debug` message stream, its output
///        classes (console, OutputDebugString and others), debug commands,
///        and the crash and exception reports that survive in the release
///        build.
///
/// The library is Zero Hour's `Libraries/Source/debug`, carried into BFME 2
/// with changes. Its centre is one `Debug` object. Code writes a report into
/// it with the stream operators, much like a `std::ostream`, and when the
/// report is finished the object hands the text to every active output
/// class. Output classes implement `DebugIOInterface`; four are registered by
/// name when the library starts:
///
/// | Name | Class | Where text goes |
/// |---|---|---|
/// | `con` | DebugIOCon | a console window, which also reads typed commands |
/// | `flat` | `DebugIOFlat` | local log files |
/// | `net` | `DebugIONet` | a named pipe |
/// | `ods` | DebugIOOds | `OutputDebugString`, for an attached debugger |
///
/// An output class does nothing until the `debug.io <name> add` command
/// creates it. Both games register all four inactive. With no command
/// file, Zero Hour runs a built-in default that adds `flat`; BFME 2's
/// default supplies no commands, so by default the release game shows its
/// reports only in the crash and exception dialogs.
///
/// \par How a report is built
/// 1. A begin call (`CrashBegin`, `CheckBegin`, `LogBegin`, or the
///    exception filter) flushes any unfinished report and calls
///    Debug::StartOutput with the report's type and its source, for
///    example `file(line)`. BFME 2's `LogBegin` is the exception: it keeps
///    an open log report from the same thread instead of flushing it.
/// 2. The stream operators turn values into text and append it with
///    Debug::AddOutput to that type's buffer.
/// 3. The matching done call ends the report; Debug::FlushOutput sends the
///    buffer to each active output class's `Write`. Log reports are the
///    exception: each completed line is flushed by Debug::AddOutput, and
///    Zero Hour's `LogDone` does not flush.
///
/// \par What still runs in the BFME 2 release build
/// Assertions, checks and logs compile out of release builds, but crash
/// reports do not. Retail code that still reports through this library
/// includes GameTextTable::readSTR (malformed string files),
/// iniParseWaterTexturesDefinition (an unknown water texture type),
/// W3DTruckDraw::updateBones (missing bones), Log_DX8_ErrorCode (Direct3D
/// errors), the debug allocator's out-of-memory path, _purecall() and the
/// exception filter. Those call sites reach the object through the global
/// pointer `theDebug` and call it through its virtual function table.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here is game data, and the library holds no game state: it has
///   no save-game code and takes no part in the logic simulation, so
///   changing what it prints cannot desynchronise a multiplayer game.
/// - Some data errors are fatal. A missing end quote or `END` in a string
///   file (GameTextTable::readSTR) and an unknown water texture type in INI
///   end the game with a "Game crash" box that names the problem; the
///   string-file message also names the label, file and line.
/// - Debug commands can be scripted without code changes. At start-up
///   `Debug::PostStaticInit` runs a command file, one command per line. It
///   names the file after the `_EA_RTS_FILENAME` environment variable if
///   that is set, else after a `-dbgcmd:<file>` command-line argument,
///   else after the game executable, with the extension replaced by
///   `.dbgcmd`; if that file does not exist it tries `default.dbgcmd`.
///   Commands have the form `group.command arguments`. Two useful ones in
///   the `debug` group:
///   - `debug.io con add` opens the console window: the `io` command
///     creates the DebugIOCon class, whose constructor opens the console;
///     extra arguments, as in `debug.io con add 120 50`, size it through
///     DebugIOCon::Execute. `flat`, `net` and `ods` are added the same way.
///   - `debug.errors +` appends the non-fatal error reports, such as
///     W3DTruckDraw::updateBones's missing-bone reports, to `errors.txt`,
///     and the game opens that file when it exits normally. It is off by
///     default, although the command's help text says on.
/// - Report text is plain English built in code. It is not looked up in
///   the string tables and cannot be translated through data.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Every output class is a Win32 seam: the console API,
///   `OutputDebugString`, files and named pipes. A port supplies its own
///   `DebugIOInterface` implementations and registers them under a name
///   with `Debug::AddIOFactory`; the report-building code above does not
///   change. BFME 2's interface has one more virtual function than Zero
///   Hour's, a yes/no query between `Read` and `Write` that only the
///   console class can answer yes (when it opened its own console).
///   BFME 2's Debug::GetInput, which Zero Hour does not have, uses it to
///   skip output classes that cannot supply typed input before it reads a
///   command line. The query's original name is not known.
/// - The library takes its memory from the Win32 global heap
///   (`GlobalAlloc`) through its own helpers (DebugFreeMemory() and its
///   siblings), except the typed-command input buffers, which
///   Debug::Update and Debug::GetInput grow with the C runtime's
///   `realloc`. None of it uses the engine's memory pools.
/// - The crash and exception dialogs are Win32 dialogs and message boxes;
///   the fatal crash path minimises the game window first.
/// - Pointers are printed as 32-bit values (Debug::operator<<(const void *),
///   memory dumps); a 64-bit port must widen them.
///
/// \see \ref sub_common_services


/// \file debug/debug_debug.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief The `Debug` object's output path: the stream operators, the
///        report buffers, build information and the exception translator.
///
/// Zero Hour's `debug_debug.cpp`. Only some of its functions are BFME 2's
/// own here; the file also keeps Zero Hour's versions of functions whose
/// BFME 2 bodies live in other files or are not reconstructed yet, so read
/// a function's page rather than the file to learn what BFME 2 does.


/// \file debug_internal.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief The library's internal assert and its memory release helper.


/// \file debug_purecall.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief The library's replacement for the C runtime's pure-virtual-call
///        handler, _purecall().


/// \class Debug
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief The debug library's central object: collects reports and debug
///        command replies and sends them to the active output classes.
///
/// \par Lifetime
/// There is one instance. In BFME 2 it is created on first use by
/// `Debug::PreStaticInit`, in memory from the library's own allocator, and
/// kept in the global pointer `theDebug`; each call to `PreStaticInit` adds
/// a reference. Zero Hour instead has a static member instance that
/// start-up code initialises before and after the C++ static constructors
/// run. `Debug::PostStaticInit` then installs the exception translator
/// (Debug::InstallExceptionHandler), registers the four output classes and
/// the `debug` command group, and runs the start-up command file described
/// in \ref grp_w1_debug_lib. Start-up code also registers
/// `Debug::StaticExit` with `atexit`; when the last reference goes, it
/// flushes the open report, deletes the active output classes and, with
/// `debug.errors` on, opens `errors.txt`.
///
/// \par One report at a time
/// The object holds one text buffer per report type and a current type.
/// Debug::StartOutput sets the type and records the report's source text;
/// Debug::FlushOutput sends the buffer and resets the type to a value
/// meaning no report. While no report is open, every stream operator
/// discards its input silently, which is how the text of a report whose
/// site is switched off is dropped. Buffers grow as needed and are always
/// terminated.
///
/// \par How the rest of the game uses it
/// - Release-build crash reports, through `theDebug`: record the report
///   site's address, `CrashBegin`, stream the message (often a
///   Debug::Format), then `CrashDone`. In BFME 2 `CrashDone` takes a mode instead of Zero Hour's
///   flag: 0 reports (with a dialog when the game runs in a window), 1 is
///   fatal (a "Game crash" box, then exit), and 2 only appends the report
///   to `errors.txt` when `debug.errors` is on.
/// - `WinMain` passes the version, build identifier and build time to
///   `Debug::SetBuildInfo`; Debug::WriteBuildInfo prints them in exception
///   reports.
/// - Debug::Update polls the output classes for typed commands.
///
/// \note BFME 2 makes most of the members virtual, the stream operators
///       included, and has one string type fewer than Zero Hour. Each source
///       file that uses the class declares only the part it needs, so
///       member names and positions differ between files; the declaration
///       Doxygen shows is Zero Hour's.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Not data-driven, apart from the debug commands (see
/// \ref grp_w1_debug_lib). Fixed limits: a Debug::Format message holds at
/// most 511 characters, and a mode-0 crash site that cannot show its
/// dialog (the game is not windowed, or dialogs are switched off) is
/// switched off after its 20th report; mode-2 sites are never switched
/// off.


/// \class Debug::Format
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief A printf-style message, formatted once into a fixed buffer so it
///        can be streamed into Debug.
///
/// Callers build a temporary from a format string and its arguments and
/// stream it into a report, as in
/// `CrashBegin(...) << Debug::Format(format, name)`.
/// Streaming it writes the buffer through Debug::operator<<(const char *).
/// Zero Hour's header marks it as a bridge for old code that should not be
/// used for new code, yet BFME 2's release-build crash reports rely on it.
///
/// \par Invariants
/// The buffer is 512 bytes and the constructor writes at most 511
/// characters; see Debug::Format::Format for the overflow case. Zero
/// Hour's declaration also disables copying.


/// \class Debug::RepeatChar
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief Streams one character a given number of times.
///
/// Holds the character and the count. Streaming it into Debug writes the
/// character that many times, in blocks of ten. The library uses it for the
/// rule lines of `=` characters that open crash and exception reports.
///
/// \note When the count is 10 or more, only whole blocks of ten are
///       written and the remainder is lost: a count of 79 writes 70
///       characters. Counts below 10 are exact. Zero Hour's operator has
///       the same arithmetic.


/// \class DebugIOCon
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief Output class `con`: writes reports to a console window and reads
///        debug commands typed into it.
///
/// Created when a debug command adds the `con` class, and destroyed by
/// `remove` or at shutdown. Zero Hour creates it in memory from the
/// library's own allocator, as BFME 2's matched factories for the other
/// output classes do. The constructor opens a console with `AllocConsole` and
/// remembers whether it did. Only a console it opened itself is read from
/// or resized: if the game already had a console, reading from it would
/// steal keystrokes from the process that owns it. The destructor frees
/// the console it opened.
///
/// BFME 2's constructor differs from Zero Hour's:
/// - it does not resize the screen buffer;
/// - it installs a console handler that claims Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Break and the
///   close event, which would otherwise end the process, and removes the
///   console window's close button;
/// - it titles the console after the executable with " [debug console]"
///   appended, even when it did not open a console.
///
/// Zero Hour: `Write` prints every report except structured command
/// replies to standard output.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Win32 console API throughout; the input line is drawn with
/// `WriteConsoleOutput` on the console's top row.


/// \class DebugIOOds
/// \ingroup grp_w1_debug_lib
/// \brief Output class `ods`: passes reports to `OutputDebugString`, for a
///        debugger or a debug-output viewer.
///
/// Write-only: it has no input and no commands of its own. Created and
/// destroyed through the `debug.io ods add` and `remove` commands like the
/// other output classes.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Windows-only (`OutputDebugString`). A replacement registers its own
/// class with Debug::AddIOFactory.


/// \fn void Debug::AddOutput(const char *str, unsigned len)
/// \brief Appends text to the buffer of the report being built.
///
/// Every stream operator ends here. Text goes to the buffer of the current
/// report type, which Debug::StartOutput chose; with no report in progress
/// the text is dropped.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The buffer grows by the text's stored length plus 1024 bytes whenever
///   fewer than 64 bytes would remain free.
/// - BFME 2 stores each line feed as a carriage return and line feed, so
///   buffers hold Windows line ends; Zero Hour stored the text unchanged.
/// - With time stamps on (the `debug.timestamp` command), the text is split
///   at line feeds and each new line starts with the local time as
///   `[hh:mm.ss.mmm] `.
/// - A log report is sent out as soon as a line is complete, and the next
///   line continues the same log report.
/// - The object remembers whether the buffer ends with a line feed;
///   Debug::FlushOutput and the other report closers use that to add a
///   final one.
///
/// \param str Text to append. Need not be terminated, but one byte past
///            the end is read and stored as the buffer's terminator when
///            the text has no line feed, or, with time stamps on, when it
///            does not end with one.
/// \param len Number of characters to append.
///
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see Debug::StartOutput, Debug::FlushOutput


/// \fn void Debug::FlushOutput(bool defaultLog)
/// \brief Sends the finished report to every active output class and
///        clears it.
///
/// Called when a report ends: by the done calls other than `LogDone`, by
/// the begin calls when another report is still unfinished, by
/// Debug::StartOutput when a log report is open, by Debug::AddOutput after
/// each complete log line, by the command executor, by the exception filter
/// and at exit.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - With no report in progress it does nothing. An empty report is
///   discarded.
/// - A final line end is added if the text does not end with one.
/// - Each active output class's `Write` gets the type, the source text and
///   the buffer. With the `debug.alwaysflush` setting on, `Write` is called
///   a second time with no text, which asks the class to flush.
/// - The buffer, the type and the source text are then reset.
///
/// \param defaultLog Ignored in BFME 2. Zero Hour: when true and no output
///                   class is active, non-release builds append the report
///                   to `default.log`.
///
/// \note With no active output class nothing receives the text. Unlike
///       Zero Hour's non-release builds, BFME 2 has no `default.log`
///       fallback; crash dialogs, `errors.txt` and the exception dialog
///       work from copies taken before the flush.
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see Debug::AddOutput, DebugIOOds::Write


/// \fn void Debug::StartOutput(DebugIOInterface::StringType type, const char *fmt, ...)
/// \brief Starts a new report of the given type and records its source
///        text.
///
/// The begin calls (`CrashBegin`, `CheckBegin`, `LogBegin`), the command
/// executor and the exception filter call it before writing anything. The
/// source text tells output classes where a report comes from, for example
/// `file(line)` for a crash or `group.command` for a command reply.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// An open log report is flushed first. The source text is formatted with
/// `wvsprintf` into a fixed 256-byte field, without a length check.
///
/// \param type Report type for the text that follows.
/// \param fmt  `wsprintf`-style format for the source text, followed by its
///             arguments.
///
/// \note Private, so this page appears only when private members are
///       extracted.
/// \see Debug::AddOutput, Debug::FlushOutput


/// \fn void Debug::WriteBuildInfo(void)
/// \brief Writes the game's version and build into the current report.
///
/// The exception filter calls it while writing an exception report, and
/// copies the text it produced for the crash dialog. The strings come from
/// `Debug::SetBuildInfo`, which `WinMain` calls with the version, the
/// build identifier and the build time.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The text is `Version:`, then the version, ` internal ` and the build
/// identifier, and ` build ` and the build time, each part only when its
/// string is not empty. The build variant word is fixed when the library is
/// compiled; the retail build writes ` release` before the build time.
///
/// \see DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(const char *str)
/// \brief Appends a string to the current report.
///
/// The basic stream operator: most others format their value and end up
/// here, and Debug::Format is written through it.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - A null pointer is written as `[NULL]`; an empty string writes nothing.
/// - A field width (Zero Hour's `Debug::Width` helper) pads the string on
///   the left with the fill character (`Debug::FillChar`, a space by
///   default) up to that width. The width applies to one insertion and is then reset,
///   except that an empty string leaves it in place.
///
/// \param str Text to write; may be null.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
/// \see Debug::AddOutput


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(const void *ptr)
/// \brief Appends a pointer as `ptr:0x` and hexadecimal digits, or
///        `ptr:NULL`.
///
/// The digits are lowercase and have no leading zeros. The radix and prefix
/// set for integers do not apply.
///
/// \param ptr Pointer value to print; only its value is used.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The value is converted to a 32-bit integer before printing.


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(float val)
/// \brief Appends a float in \%f notation, with six decimal places.
///
/// The value is formatted with `_snprintf` into a 200-byte buffer and
/// written through Debug::operator<<(const char *).
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
/// \see Debug::operator<<(double)


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(double val)
/// \brief Appends a double in \%f notation, with six decimal places.
///
/// The value is formatted with `_snprintf` into a 200-byte buffer and
/// written through Debug::operator<<(const char *).
///
/// \param val Value to print.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \warning \%f writes every integer digit, so a value of about 1e192 or
///          more fills the buffer and `_snprintf` leaves it unterminated;
///          the string written is then undefined.
/// \see Debug::operator<<(float)


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(HResult hres)
/// \brief Appends a Windows HRESULT, translated to text when a registered
///        translator recognises it.
///
/// Wrap the value as `Debug::HResult(hr)` to select this operator.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The translators added with `Debug::AddHResultTranslator` are tried in
/// order (Zero Hour keeps them sorted by priority, highest first); the
/// first one that returns true has written the text. If none does, the
/// value is written as `HResult:0x` and hexadecimal digits.
///
/// \param hres The wrapped HRESULT.
/// \return This object, for chaining.


/// \fn Debug &Debug::operator<<(const MemDump &dump)
/// \brief Appends a hex dump of a memory range, optionally with the bytes
///        as characters.
///
/// Callers describe the range with `Debug::MemDump::Raw`, `RawRel`, `Char`
/// or `CharRel`: absolute or relative addresses, with or without a
/// character column, and 1, 2 or 4 bytes per item. The exception report
/// uses it to show the code bytes around the faulting instruction.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The dump starts on a new line. Each line is an eight-digit hex address
///   (the real address, or the offset from the start) and a fixed number of
///   items: for single bytes, 21 per line without characters and 16 with.
/// - An item's bytes are printed highest address first, so a multi-byte
///   item reads as a little-endian number. Unreadable memory, found with
///   `IsBadReadPtr`, prints as `??` per byte; positions past the end are
///   blank.
/// - The character column shows bytes above the space character as they
///   are and the rest as `.`; unreadable bytes show as `?`.
///
/// \param dump Range and layout of the dump.
/// \return This object, for chaining.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Addresses are printed as 32-bit values.


/// \fn Debug::Format::Format(const char *format, ...)
/// \brief Formats a printf-style message into the object's buffer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// One `_vsnprintf` call with a limit of 511 characters.
///
/// \param format `printf` format string, followed by its arguments.
///
/// \warning A message of 511 characters or more is cut off and left
///          without a terminator, because `_vsnprintf` does not add one
///          when the text fills the limit; the buffer's last byte is never
///          set. Keep messages short.
/// \see Debug::operator<<(const char *)


/// \fn static void Debug::InstallExceptionHandler(void)
/// \brief Routes Win32 structured exceptions on the calling thread to the
///        library's exception report.
///
/// Registers LocalSETranslator() with the C runtime's `_set_se_translator`,
/// which works per thread. `Debug::PostStaticInit` calls it for the thread
/// that starts the library. Zero Hour's header asks every other thread the
/// game creates to call it as well.
///
/// \note In BFME 2 the function also fills a slot in the object's virtual
///       function table, and start-up calls it through that slot.
/// \see LocalSETranslator(), DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter


/// \fn static void LocalSETranslator(unsigned, struct _EXCEPTION_POINTERS *pExPtrs)
/// \brief The structured-exception translator that
///        Debug::InstallExceptionHandler installs.
///
/// The C runtime calls it when a Win32 structured exception meets the C++
/// exception machinery on a thread that installed it. It ignores the
/// exception code and passes the exception record and context to
/// DebugExceptionhandler::ExceptionFilter, which in BFME 2 writes the
/// exception report, may show the crash dialog, and ends the process.
///
/// \param pExPtrs Exception record and processor context of the fault.
/// \see Debug::InstallExceptionHandler


/// \fn int DebugIOCon::Read(char *buf, int maxchar)
/// \brief Returns characters of a command line typed into the console, a
///        whole line at a time, without blocking.
///
/// Debug::Update calls it repeatedly for each active output class, splits
/// what it gets at line feeds and runs each line as a debug command.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Returns nothing unless this object opened the console itself.
/// - While no line is complete, each call takes the pending key presses:
///   Enter ends the line, Backspace deletes, printable characters are added
///   up to 255. The line being typed is drawn on the console's top row in
///   its own colours with a blinking block cursor, scrolled to keep the end
///   visible.
/// - When a line is complete, the first call returns its first character
///   and later calls return the rest, up to `maxchar` at a time, until the
///   buffer is empty and typing starts again.
/// - A line of just Enter is not returned on its own: its line feed stays
///   in the buffer and comes back ahead of the next line typed, which the
///   caller then sees as an empty command.
///
/// \param buf     Receives the characters; no terminator is written.
/// \param maxchar Space in `buf`.
/// \return Number of characters stored in `buf`, 0 when none are ready.
///
/// \note BFME 2 compares the counters as signed numbers where Zero Hour
///       compared them unsigned; behaviour is the same for valid input.
/// \see DebugIOCon::Execute


/// \fn void DebugIOCon::Execute(class Debug &dbg, const char *cmd, bool structuredCmd, unsigned argn, const char *const *argv)
/// \brief Runs the console class's own commands: `help`, and `add` with an
///        optional window size.
///
/// The `debug.io con` command passes its sub-command here, after creating
/// the console on `add`. So `debug.io con add 120 50` opens the console and
/// sizes it to 120 columns by 50 rows.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - No command or `help` writes a short usage text to `dbg`.
/// - `add` with a width, and only on a console this object opened, sets
///   the window and the screen buffer to that size; the height defaults to
///   25 rows. The window size is set before and after the buffer, so both
///   growing and shrinking work.
/// - Other commands are ignored.
///
/// \param dbg           Debug object to write replies to.
/// \param cmd           Sub-command, or null.
/// \param structuredCmd True for a structured reply; not used here.
/// \param argn          Number of arguments after the sub-command.
/// \param argv          The arguments.
/// \see DebugIOCon::Read


/// \fn void DebugIOOds::Write(StringType type, const char *src, const char *str)
/// \brief Passes one report's text to `OutputDebugString`.
///
/// Debug::FlushOutput calls it for each finished report while the class is
/// active.
///
/// \param type Report type. Structured command replies are skipped; they
///             are machine-readable replies meant for tools.
/// \param src  Report source text; not used.
/// \param str  Report text, or null for a flush request, which is ignored.
///
/// \note BFME 2 has one string type fewer than Zero Hour, so the value
///       compared for structured replies is one lower; the behaviour is the
///       same.


/// \fn void DebugInternalAssert(const char *file, int line, const char *expr)
/// \brief Reports a failed internal check of the debug library itself and
///        kills the process.
///
/// Shows a message box titled "Internal assert failed" with the file, line
/// and expression, then ends the process at once with exit code 666,
/// without running any shutdown code.
///
/// Zero Hour's internal assert macro calls it only in debug builds, so the
/// release game has no caller; the function is kept in the executable all
/// the same.
///
/// \param file File name of the failed check.
/// \param line Line number of the failed check.
/// \param expr The failed expression, as text.
///
/// \warning The message is formatted into a 512-byte buffer without a
///          length check.


/// \fn void DebugFreeMemory(void *ptr)
/// \brief Frees a block from the debug library's own allocator.
///
/// The counterpart of `DebugAllocMemory` and `DebugReAllocMemory`, which
/// take memory from the Win32 global heap. The library uses them for its
/// buffers, lists, output classes and command groups, so it does not
/// depend on the engine's memory manager. Output classes use it in their
/// `Delete`, and the command executor and the frame-command queue free
/// their buffers with it.
///
/// \param ptr Block to free; null is allowed and does nothing.


/// \fn int _purecall(void)
/// \brief Reports a call to a pure virtual function as a fatal crash.
///
/// The C++ compiler fills the unimplemented slots of an abstract class's
/// virtual function table with `_purecall`, so the function runs when an
/// object is used during its construction or destruction, or after it has
/// been destroyed. BFME 2 does not import the C runtime's version: this
/// one takes its place, and every abstract class's unimplemented slots in
/// the executable point to it.
///
/// It records the address of its own report site (inside `_purecall`), so
/// all pure-virtual calls count as one report site, and writes the crash
/// report "Pure virtual function called." through `theDebug`, in the mode
/// that ends the game with the "Game crash" message box.
///
/// \return 0, which is never reached in practice.
///
/// \par Diagnosing
/// The report names no source file or line. In this fatal mode the crash
/// report includes a stack walk, which is the only lead to the caller.
